tarted    30s   kubelet            spec.containers{frontend}: Started container frontend\n', id='5c57244f-62bf-4595-98cc-45c32d5fc5f7', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b318969c6693a4f9'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3b613fe4-3635-42a2-b739-fd03fcf1bdec'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they\'ve checked the services, deployments, pods, and metrics. The frontend pods are showing some readiness issues. The metrics query showed that some pods have a condition of \'false\' or \'unknown\', but others are \'true\'. The logs and describe output for the frontend pod show it\'s running, but maybe there\'s a problem with the environment variables or dependencies.\n\nLooking at the environment variables in the pod description, there\'s a list of addresses like AD_ADDR, CART_ADDR, etc. These are probably pointing to other services. If any of these services are not available, the frontend might fail to connect, leading to readiness issues. For example, if the \'ad\' service is down, the frontend can\'t reach it, causing the pod to not be ready.\n\nThe next step is to check the status of these dependent services. Using the `get_services` tool again might help list all services, then checking each one\'s availability. Alternatively, using `get_metrics` to see if services like \'ad\', \'cart\', \'checkout\', etc., are up and running. If a service is not ready, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the logs of the dependent services might reveal if they\'re crashing or having issues. But since the user can only use the provided tools, maybe using `get_metrics` with specific queries for each service\'s readiness. For example, checking `kube_pod_status_ready` for each service. If any of them are not ready, that\'s a problem. Alternatively, using `get_traces` to see if there are any errors in the traces that point to failed calls to these services. But since the user already has the frontend pod\'s environment variables, they can cross-reference which services are supposed to be connected and check their statuses. So the next tool should be `get_services` again to list all services, then check their readiness using `get_metrics` or `kubectl` commands. Wait, but the user already used `get_services` earlier. Maybe they need to check the deployments or pods of the dependent services. Alternatively, using `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` to get the status of the dependent services. For example, checking `kubectl get pods` for the \'ad\' service. But the user might not have the exact names. Alternatively, using `get_metrics` with specific queries for each service. But the user might need to list all services first. Wait, the initial `get_services` returned [\'cart\', \'flagd\', \'frontend-proxy\'], but the `kubectl get deployments` showed more services like \'ad\', \'cart\', \'checkout\', etc. So maybe the `get_services` tool in Grafana might not list all services, but the kubectl command does. So the user should check the deployments and pods of the services mentioned in the frontend\'s environment variables. For example, check if \'ad\' service\'s pods are ready. So the next step is to use `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` to get the status of the \'ad\' service\'s pods. But the user needs to know the exact service names. Alternatively, using `get_metrics` to query the readiness of each service. For example, querying `kube_pod_status_ready{namespace=\'astronomy-shop\', pod=~\'ad-.*\'}`. If that returns \'0\' or \'unknown\', then the \'ad\' service is not ready. That would be the root cause. So the next tool call should be `get_metrics` with the appropriate query for each dependent service. But since the user can only make one tool call at a time, they need to choose one. Alternatively, using `get_traces` to see if there are any errors in the traces that indicate failed calls to dependent services. But the user might not have enough data yet. The most direct approach is to check the readiness of the dependent services using `get_metrics`. So the next tool call would be `get_metrics` with a query for each service\'s readiness. But since the user can only make one call, they might need to check one service at a time. For example, checking the \'ad\' service\'s readiness. So the tool call would be `get_metrics` with the query `kube_pod_status_ready{namespace=\'astronomy-shop\', pod=~\'ad-.*\'}`.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \nTo determine if dependent services (like `ad`, `cart`, `checkout`, etc.) are causing the frontend readiness issues, I need to check their pod readiness status. The `get_metrics` tool with a query targeting `kube_pod_status_ready` for these services will reveal if any are not ready, which could explain the frontend\'s failure to establish connections.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_metrics", "arguments": {"query": "kube_pod_status_ready{namespace=\'astronomy-shop\', pod=~\'ad|cart|checkout|currency|product-catalog|product-reviews|recommendation|shipping|flagd\'}"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1038, 'prompt_tokens': 4886, 'total_tokens': 5924, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbda-9594-71a1-95ed-3a8d1ccf32da-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4886, 'output_tokens': 1038, 'total_tokens': 5924}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4f5ae56-b0f3-4853-85cc-3d955fc0d893'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which consists of many microservices. The main problem seems to be with the frontend service, as indicated by the previous tool calls showing that the frontend pods are not ready. \n\nFirst, the user started by getting the list of services using `get_services`, which returned cart, flagd, and frontend-proxy. Then they checked the deployments and pods, noticing that the frontend has two pods, but one of them is not ready. The metrics query for the frontend pods showed some conflicting states—some entries had 'condition' as 'false' or 'unknown', which suggests that the pods might not be ready or there's an issue with their readiness probes.\n\nNext, the user checked the logs and describe output for the frontend pod, which showed that the pod is running, but the readiness probes might be failing. The environment variables in the pod's configuration include addresses for other services like ad, cart, checkout, etc. If any of these services are not available, the frontend might fail to connect, leading to readiness issues.\n\nThe user then queried the readiness status of other services (ad, cart, checkout, etc.) using `get_metrics`. The response from the tool call needs to be analyzed to see if any of these services are not ready. If any of them are not ready, that could be the root cause because the frontend depends on them. For example, if the ad service is down, the frontend might not be able to reach it, causing the readiness probe to fail.\n\nLooking at the metrics query result, if any of the services listed (ad, cart, checkout, etc.) have a readiness status of 'false' or 'unknown', that would indicate a problem. The user needs to check each of these services to see if they are up and running. If a service is not ready, the next step would be to investigate why that service is not functioning correctly—maybe it's a deployment issue, a network problem, or a configuration error.\n\nAdditionally, the frontend's environment variables might have incorrect hostnames or ports pointing to these services. For instance, if the `AD_ADDR` is set to `ad:8080` but the actual service is running on a different port or hostname, the frontend would fail to connect. Checking the service configurations and ensuring that all dependencies are correctly referenced is crucial.\n\nAnother angle is to look at the network policies or firewall rules that might be blocking communication between the frontend and the dependent services. Using tools like `get_dependency_graph` could help visualize the service interactions and identify any missing or broken connections.\n\nIf the metrics show that all dependent services are ready, the issue might be with the frontend's readiness probe configuration. The user could check the probe's endpoint, timeout, and interval settings to ensure they are correctly set up. If the probe is too aggressive or the endpoint is not responding, the pod might mark itself as not ready even if the service is functional.\n\nIn summary, the next step is to check the readiness status of all dependent services. If any are not ready, investigate their specific issues. If they are ready, then the problem might be with the frontend's configuration or readiness probe settings. The user should also verify the network connectivity between the frontend and the other services to rule out any communication issues.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"service": "frontend", "last_n_minutes": 5}', 'name': 'get_traces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bba177c127d26c00', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 723, 'prompt_tokens': 5923, 'total_tokens': 6646, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbda-c6a4-74c0-8c14-4c1221edcf38-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_traces', 'args': {'service': 'frontend', 'last_n_minutes': 5}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bba177c127d26c00', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5923, 'output_tokens': 723, 'total_tokens': 6646}), ToolMessage(content='[{\'traceID\': \'efdbee7921773be8cca015068adfbe7c\', \'spans\': [{\'traceID\': \'efdbee7921773be8cca015068adfbe7c\', \'spanID\': \'52fa82bd9f59b50e\', \'operationName\': \'postgresql\', \'references\': [{\'refType\': \'CHILD_OF\', \'traceID\': \'efdbee7921773be8cca015068adfbe7c\', \'spanID\': \'28756e2eac979edd\'}], \'startTime\': 1773751936873470, \'duration\': 1397, \'tags\': [{\'key\': \'db.system.name\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'postgresql\'}, {\'key\': \'db.query.text\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'\\n\\t\\tSELECT p.id, p.name, p.description, p.picture, \\n\\t\\t       p.price_currency_code, p.price_units, p.price_nanos, p.categories\\n\\t\\tFROM catalog.products p\\n\\t\\tWHERE p.id = $1\\n\\t\'}, {\'key\': \'unsanitized_span_name\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'sql.conn.query\'}, {\'key\': \'span.kind\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'client\'}, {\'key\': \'internal.span.format\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'otlp\'}], \'logs\': [], \'processID\': \'p1\', \'warnings\': None}, {\'traceID\': \'efdbee7921773be8cca015068adfbe7c\', \'spanID\': \'28756e2eac979edd\', \'operationName\': \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService/GetProduct\', \'references\': [{\'refType\': \'CHILD_OF\', \'traceID\': \'efdbee7921773be8cca015068adfbe7c\', \'spanID\': \'7f228fa9629f2f5c\'}], \'startTime\': 1773751936873285, \'duration\': 1883, \'tags\': [{\'key\': \'rpc.service\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService\'}, {\'key\': \'rpc.method\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'GetProduct\'}, {\'key\': \'rpc.system\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'grpc\